# Code owners — who must review a change to the files CI trusts.
#
# ## What this is for
#
# Every privacy and security guarantee in this repo is enforced by an artifact
# that lives IN the repo: the privacy-invariant manifest, the repo guards and
# their fixtures, the workflows that run them, the wire allow-list, the declared
# skip manifests. That is what makes them checkable — and it is also the attack
# surface. A change that edits the manifest, loosens a guard, or unwires a
# workflow step makes CI agree with itself while the guarantee is gone. Nothing
# in CI can catch that, because CI is the thing being edited. `docs/privacy/
# README.md` records it as the limit of the gate ("what the gate cannot prove");
# a human reviewer on those paths is the only mitigation there is.
#
# ## This file does NOTHING on its own
#
# CODEOWNERS only auto-requests review. It becomes a REQUIREMENT solely through
# branch protection on `main` — see the OWNER ACTION block at the foot of this
# file. Until that is switched on, treat these paths as unprotected.
#
# ## Syntax notes that matter here
#
#   * The LAST matching pattern wins, so the broad default comes first and the
#     security-critical paths come after it. That ordering is the point: if a
#     second maintainer is ever added to `*`, the specific rules below keep the
#     enforcement surface reviewed by its owner rather than silently widening
#     with the team.
#   * A leading `/` anchors to the repo root; a trailing `/` matches everything
#     beneath the directory.
#   * An owner GitHub cannot resolve makes the rule inert, not red. If the
#     handle below ever changes, this file must change with it — see OWNER
#     ACTION step 6.

