# ===========================================================================
# OWNER ACTION — required, and not doable from a commit
#
# GitHub → repo Settings → Rules → Rulesets → New branch ruleset (or the
# classic Settings → Branches → Add branch protection rule), targeting `main`:
#
#   1. Require a pull request before merging.
#      Without this, a direct push to `main` bypasses CODEOWNERS entirely and
#      every rule above is decoration.
#   2. Under that rule, tick "Require review from Code Owners".
#      This is the switch that makes this file mean anything.
#   3. Required approvals: 1.
#   4. Block force pushes, and block branch deletion.
#   5. Do NOT leave a bypass list, and (classic UI) tick "Include
#      administrators" / "Do not allow bypassing the above settings".
#      An admin-exempt rule protects against everyone except the account a
#      poisoning attempt would be using.
#   6. Recommended alongside: "Require status checks to pass" with the ci.yml
#      jobs selected, and "Require branches to be up to date before merging",
#      so a guard cannot be edited on a stale base that never ran it.
#
# Honest limit for a solo repo: a code owner cannot approve their OWN pull
# request, so with steps 1-3 and no bypass the owner cannot self-merge. Pick
# one deliberately:
#
#   * add a second trusted maintainer as a reviewer (best — it is what the
#     rule is for), or
#   * keep the rules on and use the admin override for your own PRs, which at
#     least makes each bypass an explicit, logged act rather than the default.
#
# Either way the protection that matters lands immediately: no pull request
# from anyone else can change the manifest, a guard, a workflow, the wire
# allow-list or the security-critical Rust without the owner's review.
# ===========================================================================
